---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/title.jpg";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="Three figures in yellow hazmat suits walking away down a sickly-lit, featureless corridor lined with identical carpeted walls"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <section class="hook">
    <p class="hook-lede">You have probably encountered an anomaly already.</p>
    <p>You may not have called it that.</p>
    <ul class="hook-examples">
      <li>A corridor that was longer on the way back.</li>
      <li>A classroom that appeared to be occupied after everyone had left.</li>
      <li>A reflection that moved a fraction too late.</li>
      <li>A voice from somewhere you were certain was empty.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Most anomalous phenomena are not dramatic. They occur briefly, leave
      incomplete evidence, and are easily explained away.
    </p>
    <p class="hook-emphasis">This is precisely why they require training.</p>
    <p>
      <strong>
        {courseMeta.code}: {courseMeta.title}
      </strong> teaches students how to observe, classify, investigate, and
      respond to anomalous phenomena in ordinary campus environments.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because when something impossible happens in the middle of an
      otherwise normal day, you still have a class to get to.
    </p>
  </section>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    Log, corroborate and classify anomalous phenomena using this course's
    observational method. Run two supervised on-campus observations under
    the fieldwork safety protocol. Survey a regional and a global corpus of
    documented cases as comparative desk study, then evaluate the competing
    theoretical frameworks currently offered for what those cases converge
    on. You leave with a case file, a comparative literature review, and a
    containment/warding protocol for a novel case — the method, exercised
    end to end, on cases you're expected to recognize from your own life.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Students who can log an observation without interpreting it as they go,
    and who can treat a protective ritual, a contested causal theory, or a
    widely-circulated report as evidence to be weighed rather than folklore
    to be dismissed. No prior anomalology is assumed. A working
    comparative-method habit from any empirical discipline transfers
    directly — the phenomena are new to the discipline; the discipline of
    observing them isn't.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title="Timetable" href="/timetable/">
      <p>The whole semester at a glance: phase, practical and lecture for every week.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The weekly schedule and what happens in each teaching practical.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .hook {
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
  }

  .hook-lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .hook-emphasis {
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .hook-examples {
    margin-block: 0.5rem 1rem;
    padding-inline-start: 1.25rem;
  }

  .hook-examples li {
    margin-block-end: 0.35rem;
  }
</style>
